"The Journey of a Water Drop"
The water in our rivers, lakes and seas is always moving. When the sun heats the sea, tiny drops of water change into water vapour and rise into the air. This is called evaporation. High above the ground the air is cool, so the vapour turns back into tiny droplets that join together to form clouds. This is called condensation. When the droplets become too heavy, they fall to the ground as rain, or as snow in very cold places. Rainwater flows into streams and rivers and finally reaches the sea again. This never-ending journey is called the water cycle. Because of the water cycle, the same water has been used again and again for millions of years.

