"Ship of the Desert"
The camel is often called the 'ship of the desert' because it can carry people and goods across hot, sandy deserts for long distances. A camel can go without drinking water for many days. When it finally finds water, it can drink a very large amount in a few minutes. Its hump stores fat, not water as many people think. The fat gives the camel energy when food is hard to find. Camels have long eyelashes and can close their nostrils to keep out blowing sand. Their wide, padded feet do not sink into the soft sand. In Rajasthan, camels are still used by many families for farming, transport and even for racing at fairs.

