"Food from Sunlight"
Animals have to find their food, but green plants make their own. Plants take in water from the soil through their roots. They take in a gas called carbon dioxide from the air through tiny holes in their leaves. The green colouring in leaves, called chlorophyll, uses the energy of sunlight to turn water and carbon dioxide into food. This process is called photosynthesis. While making food, plants give out oxygen, which humans and animals need to breathe. That is why forests are sometimes called the lungs of the Earth. Without green plants, there would be no food for animals and no fresh oxygen in the air.

