"The Postman's Bicycle"
Hari Kaka was the postman of three villages. For thirty years he rode an old bicycle on dusty roads, carrying letters, money orders and parcels. People waited for him eagerly, because a letter could bring news of a wedding, a new job or a newborn baby. Many old people could not read, so Hari Kaka often read the letters aloud to them and even helped them write replies. When mobile phones came, fewer letters were sent, but Hari Kaka's work did not stop. He now delivered bank documents, school books and medicines. On the day he retired, the villagers gave him a new bicycle with a bell that rang loudly, and everyone clapped.

